# ---------------------------------------------------------------- Big picture (EN)
BIG_PICTURE_EN = '''      <p class="lead">Ten weeks on, the war has stopped being a windfall and become a cost. The Ministry of Finance's July–September report shows oil income down 23% because the Kingdom could not ship enough — the gap for the first nine months already equals the whole year-end estimate. Yet corporate Saudi Arabia is recovering faster than the state: Aramco's cash covers its dividend again, Petro Rabigh and SABIC are back in profit, Acwa's profit jumped 71%. The front line moved to EH's own coast and corridor — the East–West pipeline, Yanbu, Rabigh, even Aramco's headquarters — and the regulator turned the waste opportunity into enforceable, biddable demand: 6,449 violations in a quarter, SAR 5 million marine fines, and MWAN's first priced slate of 27 facilities.</p>
      <div class="hl-grid">
        <div class="hl-item">
          <h4>The war is now a cost, not a windfall</h4>
          <p>April–June's smaller gap was a one-off. In July–September oil income fell 23% while Brent averaged about $91, because the barrels were missing; the nine-month gap is SAR 244 billion, 148% of the plan, and 2027 is budgeted with a SAR 191 billion gap financed by borrowing. Spending did fall 4% in the quarter — the first restraint of the war — so government buyers will fund compliance and must-do infrastructure, not optional studies. The full arithmetic is on the hub's Government Finances page.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="hl-item">
          <h4>Companies are recovering faster than the state</h4>
          <p>Aramco earned $31.2 billion and its spare cash covered the dividend; Petro Rabigh made SAR 2.6 billion in a quarter; SABIC swung to profit; Acwa's profit rose 71% — thanks to refining margins and rerouted logistics. EH's industrial clients have money and growing maintenance budgets; its government clients are stretched. Marafiq's 41% profit fall is the reminder that Jubail still runs below normal.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="hl-item">
          <h4>The front line moved to our coast</h4>
          <p>Drones from Iraq shut the East–West pipeline for two weeks; the Houthis took the islands at the Red Sea's southern gate, then lost the Mokha coast to government forces; missiles reached Aramco's Dhahran headquarters; Yanbu, Rabigh, Ras Tanura and Abqaiq were claimed targets, and civil-defence alerts reached Jeddah. Spill response, remediation and emergency planning are now a live, local need — on the Gulf coast as well as the Red Sea.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="hl-item">
          <h4>Hormuz is open but dangerous; Bab el-Mandeb is contested</h4>
          <p>Oil flows are near pre-war levels through escorted corridors — October was the strongest export month since February — but Iran keeps the strait formally closed, October saw the most ship attacks of the war, and the US struck Iranian air defences on 6–7 November while talks continued. Plan for a long period of elevated maritime risk with gradual Gulf-coast restarts.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="hl-item hl-full">
          <h4>Regulation is doing the market's work</h4>
          <p>NCEC's 6,449 quarterly violations and SAR 33 million in fines, marine-pollution fines of up to SAR 5 million with mandatory spill reporting, Riyadh's dust enforcement, 17,000 permits issued this year, and MWAN's priced SAR 2.1 billion slate of 33 facilities turn the "$112 billion opportunity" into enforceable, biddable demand — the strongest structural tailwind for EH's permitting, monitoring, medical-waste and treatment services. The new procurement law (direct purchases up to SAR 1 million, four-day Ministry review, pay existing suppliers first) changes how that demand is bought from around the turn of the year.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="hl-item hl-full">
          <h4>PIF's strategy is reshaping who EH competes with — and partners with</h4>
          <p>Waste and recycling (SIRC) now sit in a named PIF ecosystem beside Acwa, and PIF wants private operators to build. At the same time Veolia signed its way into Acwa, Ma'aden and GASCO's storage arm in a single day, and HUMAIN's data-centre build-out has Aramco's balance sheet behind it. EH's route to scale is as a regional partner and specialist subcontractor on these platforms — in Makkah, Madinah and the Red Sea corridor first.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="hl-item hl-full">
          <h4 class="blue">We fact-check ourselves — one claim contradicted, four corrections</h4>
          <p>All 24 claims from our 26 August report were re-checked: eight held unchanged, twelve were overtaken by events, three needed clarifying, and one — Hormuz traffic "about a tenth of normal" — was contradicted by October's recovery. Four corrections are listed above (a SABIC loss figure, the PIF construction numbers, a stale Alujain result, the Hormuz claim), and one earlier gap is closed (MWAN's opportunities). We also list what we could not find: competitor contract wins, health-cluster medical-waste tenders, Sadara, and any confirmed Red Sea spill.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="hl-item hl-full">
          <h4>What to watch, November–December</h4>
          <p><b>11 November</b> — stock-market deadline for nine-month results (Ma'aden, Alujain, Saudi Electricity, Luberef; any Jubail restart language). <b>29 November</b> — OPEC+ meeting, then Aramco's December price for Asia. <b>December</b> — the 2027 budget statement (no date announced); MEWA, NCEC and municipal allocations. <b>Around the turn of the year</b> — the new procurement law takes effect; local-content rules still to be issued. <b>1 January 2027</b> — NCEC deadline to apply for fine instalments, likely a permit-application rush. <b>MWAN's 27 opportunities</b> — platform, deadlines and pre-qualification, especially the Madinah and Dammam medical-waste packages. <b>The Red Sea front</b> — attack tempo around Yanbu, Rabigh and Jeddah; the Jazan restart; Türkiye's ratification vote. <b>Year-end</b> — Red Sea Global phase one, Round 7 awards, Qiddiya District 0, and whether the Ras Mohaisen–Baha–Makkah water line is confirmed for 2027.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
'''

